@using DYS.Molargo.Shared.Features.Admin.ViewModels

@*
    The design's roles pane: roles on the left, a per-module read/write permission matrix
    on the right.

    The matrix is not built, and this is the pane where building it as decoration would do
    the most harm. A grid showing "Reception · Clinical notes · read-only" is a claim that
    the app enforces it. It does not — there is no sign-in, so there is nobody to enforce
    it against, and a practice manager who saw that grid would reasonably stop worrying
    about who can open a record.

    What is shown instead is the truth: the two behaviours a role really governs, and the
    staff currently holding it.
*@

<div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-[1fr_1.3fr]">
    <div class="border-divider px-6 py-5 lg:border-r-2">
        <h5 class="mt-0 mb-1">Roles</h5>
        <p class="mt-0 mb-3 text-xs text-ink/55">
            Pick a role to see what it changes.
        </p>

        <div class="flex flex-col items-start gap-1.5">
            @foreach (var role in AdminViewModel.Roles)
            {
                <button type="button" class="@ChipCss.Chip(Vm.SelectedRole == role)"
                        @onclick="() => Vm.SelectRoleCommand.Execute(role)">
                    @AdminViewModel.RoleLabel(role)
                </button>
            }
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="flex flex-col gap-3 px-6 py-5">
        <h5 class="m-0">@AdminViewModel.RoleLabel(Vm.SelectedRole)</h5>

        <div class="border-2 border-accent bg-accent-100 p-3 text-sm text-accent-800"
             role="alert">
            <strong>Roles grant no access.</strong>
            There is no permission model and no sign-in, so a role cannot stop anyone
            opening anything. What it does is decide what a person can be recorded as
            having done.
        </div>

        <div class="flex flex-col text-[13px]">
            <div class="flex items-center justify-between gap-3 border-b border-divider py-2.5">
                <span>
                    Author clinical records
                    <span class="text-[11px] text-ink/55">
                        notes, prescriptions, certificates
                    </span>
                </span>
                <span class="@Verdict(Vm.SelectedRoleIsClinical)">
                    @(Vm.SelectedRoleIsClinical ? "Allowed" : "Refused")
                </span>
            </div>

            <div class="flex items-center justify-between gap-3 border-b border-divider py-2.5">
                <span>
                    Be booked in the diary
                    <span class="text-[11px] text-ink/55">gets a chair column</span>
                </span>
                <span class="@Verdict(Vm.SelectedRoleIsBookable)">
                    @(Vm.SelectedRoleIsBookable ? "Allowed" : "Refused")
                </span>
            </div>

            <div class="flex items-center justify-between gap-3 border-b border-divider py-2.5">
                <span>
                    Sign a medical certificate
                    <span class="text-[11px] text-ink/55">
                        needs a current professional registration as well
                    </span>
                </span>
                <span class="@Verdict(Vm.SelectedRoleIsClinical)">
                    @(Vm.SelectedRoleIsClinical ? "If registered" : "Refused")
                </span>
            </div>

            <div class="flex items-center justify-between gap-3 border-b border-divider py-2.5">
                <span>
                    Open any patient record
                    <span class="text-[11px] text-ink/55">
                        including notes, documents and balances
                    </span>
                </span>
                @* The row that matters, and the honest answer is the same for every role. *@
                <span class="text-xs font-bold text-accent-800">Anyone can</span>
            </div>

            <div class="flex items-center justify-between gap-3 py-2.5">
                <span>
                    Change or delete records
                    <span class="text-[11px] text-ink/55">
                        subject to each screen's own guards
                    </span>
                </span>
                <span class="text-xs font-bold text-accent-800">Anyone can</span>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="bg-surface p-3 text-[13px]">
            <strong>@Vm.SelectedRoleStaffCount</strong>
            @(Vm.SelectedRoleStaffCount == 1 ? "person holds" : "people hold") this role.
            <div class="mt-1 text-xs text-ink/70">
                What protects records today is each screen's own rules — a signed note
                cannot be edited, an issued invoice cannot be altered, a chart finding is
                superseded rather than deleted. Those apply to everyone equally, because
                the app cannot tell who is using it.
            </div>
        </div>

        <p class="m-0 text-xs text-ink/55">
            @*
                Named so the gap is a known quantity rather than an oversight. The design's
                per-field claims are specific enough to be worth quoting back.
            *@
            The design also promises per-field control — reception editing demographics but
            not medical history, fee columns hidden from assistants, Medicare numbers masked
            except for billing roles. All of that needs sign-in, a permission model, and
            every screen to consult it.
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public AdminViewModel Vm { get; set; } = default!;

    /// <summary>
    /// A permission verdict.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Whole literal strings, both branches — Tailwind scans source text, so a class
    /// assembled from a base plus a conditional suffix never reaches the stylesheet.
    /// </remarks>
    private static string Verdict(bool allowed) => allowed
        ? "text-xs font-semibold text-ink"
        : "text-xs text-ink/55";
}
